.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt RECV 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm recv ,
.Nm recvfrom ,
.Nm recvmsg ,
.Nm recvmmsg
.Nd receive socket data
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn recv "int fd" "void *buf" "size_t len" "int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn recvfrom "int fd" "void *restrict buf" "size_t len" "int flags" "struct sockaddr *restrict addr" "socklen_t *restrict addrlen"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn recvmsg "int fd" "struct msghdr *msg" "int flags"
.Pp
.Fd #define _GNU_SOURCE
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn recvmmsg "int fd" "struct mmsghdr *msgvec" "unsigned int vlen" "int flags" "struct timespec *timeout"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn recv
and
.Fn recvfrom
copy up to
.Fa len
bytes from socket
.Fa fd
to
.Fa buf .
A stream receive returns available bytes and may return less than requested.
After a peer's FIN, buffered data is returned before end of input.
A datagram receive takes one message and discards bytes beyond the
buffer capacity.
A zero-length datagram is consumed and returns zero.
A zero-capacity datagram receive also consumes the message.
.Pp
An empty receive waits unless
.Dv MSG_DONTWAIT
is set in
.Fa flags
or the descriptor has
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
set.
These nonblocking forms return
.Er EAGAIN
when no input is available and the stream has not ended.
A signal may interrupt a wait with
.Er EINTR .
.Pp
.Dv MSG_PEEK
copies what the receive would take and leaves it for the next receive:
the bytes at the front of a stream, which do not change the window the
connection offers, or the waiting datagram whole, with its sender.
A datagram is received whole, up to the largest a socket of its kind
takes: 65507 bytes of UDP over IPv4, 65527 over IPv6, a whole IP datagram
on a raw socket and a frame's payload on a packet socket.
A datagram longer than the buffer is cut, and the rest of it dropped;
.Fn recvmsg
says so with
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
in
.Fa msg_flags .
On a datagram socket,
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
in
.Fa flags
makes the call return the datagram's whole length, even when its buffers
took less, as Linux does; netlink's programs ask it with
.Dv MSG_PEEK
and no buffer to learn how large a buffer to give.
Other flag bits are ignored:
.Dv MSG_WAITALL
does not wait to fill the buffer.
.Pp
A datagram socket queues what it receives within its receive budget,
.Dv SO_RCVBUF ,
212992 bytes unless set, each datagram's own record counted with it; a
datagram that arrives with the budget spent is dropped, as Linux drops
it, and counted in RcvbufErrors of
.Pa /proc/net/snmp
for UDP over IPv4.
A netlink socket's answers come in datagrams of at most 3776 bytes, or
once it has read with a larger buffer, as large as that buffer and at most
32448 bytes, as Linux packs a dump.
On an
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket these flags behave as in Linux, and
.Fn recvmsg
returns the control messages, which
.Xr unix 7
describes.
The direct calls
.Fn recvfrom ,
number 371, and
.Fn recvmsg ,
number 372, take their arguments in registers.
.Ss Source addresses
.Fn recvfrom
reports a datagram's sender when
.Fa addr
is non-null.
Before the call,
.Pf * Fa addrlen
is the buffer capacity in bytes.
The address is truncated to that capacity and the full address length
is written to
.Pf * Fa addrlen .
A null
.Fa addr
asks for no address, and
.Fa addrlen
is not read.
A non-null
.Fa addr
with a null
.Fa addrlen
fails with
.Er EFAULT
before any data is taken, so the message stays queued for the next
receive; Linux fails the same call only after it has taken the message,
which is then lost.
On a TCP stream, supplied address buffers are checked but no source
address or length is written.
If receiving the data fails, no source address or length is written.
.Pp
A receive that waits checks the data buffer again when the wait ends,
since another thread may have unmapped it meanwhile.
A buffer that can no longer be written fails the call with
.Er EFAULT ,
and the datagram or the stream bytes stay queued for the next receive.
.Pp
An IPv4 socket returns a 16-byte
.Vt sockaddr_in
with its sender's address and port in network byte order; a raw ICMP
receive reports the address with the port zero.
An IPv6 UDP socket returns a 28-byte sockaddr_in6, with mapped IPv4
addresses for IPv4 senders and a scope id for link-scoped IPv6 senders.
recvmsg and recvmmsg preserve the same address and scope across their iovecs.
A packet socket returns a 20-byte
.Vt sockaddr_ll
with the sender's hardware address, protocol, interface, hardware type,
and packet type.
Packet-socket data omits the Ethernet header.
A raw ICMP receive includes the IPv4 header, of the datagram reassembled
when it came in fragments.
A raw ICMPv6 socket returns a 28-byte
.Vt sockaddr_in6
with the sender's address and, for a link-local sender, the index of the
interface it came in on as its scope id; its data is the ICMPv6 message
without the IPv6 header, as on Linux.
An ioctl-only raw socket has no receive operation.
.Ss Scatter receives
.Fn recvmmsg ,
subcall 19 and the direct call 337, receives up to
.Fa vlen
messages, at most 1024, into the headers at
.Fa msgvec
as
.Fn recvmsg
receives one, storing each message's count in its
.Va msg_len ,
and returns how many it received, or the first error when none came.
Each receive waits as the socket's would; with
.Dv MSG_WAITFORONE
only the first does, and the rest take what has already come.
With a
.Fa timeout ,
the call ends after the message during which the time ran out, and
.Fa timeout
is given back the time that remains, zero once it has run out, when a
message came; as on Linux, the timeout does not end a wait for a
message.
A timeout with negative seconds or nanoseconds past a second fails with
.Er EINVAL .
On i686
.Fa timeout
is a
.Vt struct timespec
of 32-bit words, and the call
.Nm recvmmsg_time64 ,
417, takes Linux's
.Vt struct __kernel_timespec
of 64-bit words instead, the high word of its nanoseconds ignored.
.Pp
Among Internet and packet sockets,
.Fn recvmsg
takes
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM
and
.Dv SOCK_RAW
sockets, packet sockets included, and rejects stream sockets and the
raw descriptor of
.Dv IPPROTO_RAW
that takes only ioctls; the rest of this section describes those.
On an
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket of any type it receives data, the sender's name and the control
messages
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
and
.Dv SCM_CREDENTIALS ,
and sets
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
and
.Dv MSG_CTRUNC
in
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_flags ,
as
.Xr unix 7
describes.
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_iov
and
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_iovlen
describe up to 1024 buffers, filled in order from exactly one datagram.
Any remainder beyond their capacity is discarded.
Each nonempty buffer must have at most 2147483647 bytes.
A list with no capacity still consumes a datagram, unless
.Dv MSG_PEEK
is set, which leaves it queued as for
.Fn recvfrom .
.Pp
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_name
receives the sender in the same form as
.Fn recvfrom .
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_namelen
is the input capacity and receives the full address length even when
the address is truncated.
It receives zero when
.Va msg_name
is null.
The message header, vector entries, nonempty data buffers, and the
portion of the address buffer to be written are checked before input
is consumed.
.Pp
A UDP, raw or packet socket, or an AF_UNIX one as
.Xr unix 7
says, that asked for it with
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMP
or
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMPNS
.Pq Xr setsockopt 2
receives first, at level
.Dv SOL_SOCKET
and with the option's number as its type, the time the datagram was
queued by the realtime clock:
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMP_OLD
(29) a
.Vt struct timeval
and
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMPNS_OLD
(35) a
.Vt struct timespec ,
each two of the caller's longs, two 32-bit words for an i386 program on
the x86-64 kernel;
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMP_NEW
(63) and
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMPNS_NEW
(64) two 64-bit words in every ABI.
.Pp
An IPv4 raw or ICMP datagram socket that asked for it with
.Dv IP_RECVTTL
.Pq Xr setsockopt 2
receives the datagram's TTL, an integer, at level
.Dv IPPROTO_IP
and type
.Dv IP_TTL .
Any other IPv4 socket, and a packet socket, supplies no other ancillary
data, and one that asked for none supplies none:
the control buffer is unused and
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_controllen
is set to zero, and
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_flags
is
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
when the datagram was longer than the buffers and zero otherwise.
.Pp
An AF_INET6 UDP or raw socket that asked for them with
.Dv IPV6_RECVPKTINFO ,
.Dv IPV6_RECVHOPLIMIT
or
.Dv IPV6_RECVTCLASS
.Pq Xr setsockopt 2
receives, at level
.Dv IPPROTO_IPV6
and in this order,
.Dv IPV6_PKTINFO ,
a
.Vt struct in6_pktinfo
of the datagram's destination and the index of the interface it came in
on;
.Dv IPV6_HOPLIMIT ,
its hop limit; and
.Dv IPV6_TCLASS ,
its traffic class, each an integer.
An IPv4 datagram received on a dual-stack socket has its destination,
mapped, and interface 0, and neither of the others.
The control buffer is checked before the datagram is taken.
A message that does not fit whole is not written, nor is any after it,
and
.Dv MSG_CTRUNC
is set in
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_flags ;
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_controllen
receives the bytes written, their padding counted as far as the buffer
goes.
.Ss Kernel interface
These operations use
.Xr socketcall 2
(i386 system call 102): operation 10 for
.Nm recv ,
12 for
.Nm recvfrom ,
and 17 for
.Nm recvmsg .
Musl implements
.Fn recv
through
.Fn recvfrom
with null address pointers.
The direct kernel operation 10 accepts streams, datagram and raw
sockets, and rejects only the ioctl-only raw descriptor.
.Pp
x86-64 has no
.Nm recv
call: musl's
.Fn recv
calls
.Nm recvfrom
(45) with no address.
.Nm recvmsg
is 47 and
.Nm recvmmsg
299; their
.Vt struct msghdr
has the x86-64 layout, with 64-bit lengths, and control messages are
aligned to 8 bytes.
An i386 program on the x86-64 kernel passes the i386 layout, with 32-bit
pointers and lengths and control messages aligned to 4 bytes, through
the i386 numbers or
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
and gets it back, as on Linux.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success,
.Fn recv ,
.Fn recvfrom
and
.Fn recvmsg
return the bytes copied, and
.Fn recvmmsg
the number of messages received, with each message's count of bytes in
its
.Va msg_len .
Zero bytes can mean stream end of input, an empty datagram, or a receive
with no buffer capacity.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel operations return a negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
sends
.Ql loopback-udp
to a UDP socket through
.Li 127.0.0.1
and receives it with
.Fn recvfrom .
It checks the payload and the reported source address and port.
It then gives a
.Fn recvfrom
an address and no length pointer, requires
.Er EFAULT ,
and receives the datagram that call left queued;
.Pa tests/musl/unixmusl.c
does the same on an
.Dv AF_UNIX
datagram pair.
The same probe peeks at a datagram with
.Dv MSG_PEEK ,
twice, before a receive takes it, and peeks at one byte of a loopback
TCP connection before a read takes the whole message from that byte.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ECONNREFUSED
.It Er EAGAIN
No message or stream data is available for a nonblocking receive.
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Er ECONNREFUSED
A stream connection was refused, or the peer of a connected datagram socket
answered an earlier datagram with a port unreachable.
.It Er ECONNRESET
A stream connection was reset, or a closed stream with no buffered data,
no received FIN and no recorded error was read.
.It Er EFAULT
The socketcall arguments, data buffers, message header, vector list,
or address buffers cannot be accessed as required, or
.Fn recvfrom
was given an address and a null length pointer.
For recv and recvfrom, a length exceeding 2147483647 also gives this error;
recvmsg gives it for an individual buffer exceeding that size.
.It Er EINTR
A signal interrupted the wait.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa msg Ns ->msg_iovlen
exceeds 1024.
.It Er ENETUNREACH
A stream connection timed out while its local address was gone.
.It Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
does not refer to a socket.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
An ioctl-only socket was passed to kernel operation 10 or to recvfrom, or an IPv4 socket other than
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM
was passed to recvmsg.
.It Er ENOTCONN
The stream has no TCP control block and has not been shut down.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
A stream connection timed out.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
